{
  "description": "issue #738 (maintainer ruling) + issue #679 (SP7) + issue #847: pins `clear_screen` when MORE THAN ONE turtle is addressed — the case SP5's single-turtle clear-screen-homes-addressed-turtle left open. There is one shared drawing surface, so it is cleared ONCE however many turtles are addressed (spec/turtles-and-sprites.md:111): `clear_screen` is deliberately NOT a per-turtle command, because multiplying the whole statement would emit N `clear` events for one canvas clear. Its HOMING is nonetheless ordinary per-turtle movement and applies once for each addressed turtle (:113), so under `tell [ :a :b ]` BOTH turtles return home. The stream carries one issue #847 homing pair per addressed turtle — `move` ([0 10] -> [0 0], reporting the pre-reset heading 45) then `turn` (45 -> 0), each stamped with that turtle's own `turtle_id` — followed by the single `clear`. No `draw-segment` accompanies either homing move even though both pens are down: the same instruction goes on to clear the drawing (spec/rendering.md:36 describes what a pen-down move contributes to the RETAINED SCENE, and :150's \"Clear operations\" row fixes `clear_screen`'s contribution at *cleared*; escalated to the maintainer as issue #858). The `clear` itself carries NO `turtle_id` — :113: \"A `clear` event describes the shared surface rather than any turtle, so it is not turtle-specific and carries no turtle identity\" — because one shared-surface event cannot name the N turtles one `clear_screen` homes; a consumer reads the homing from the per-turtle `move`/`turn` events and MUST NOT read the `clear` as an instruction to move a turtle. The trailing `each` proves the outcome rather than just the events: BOTH turtles report [0 0] and heading 0. This fixture REPLACES clear-screen-multi-turtle-homes-current, whose name and expectation recorded the previous behaviour — one `clear` stamped `turtle_id: 1`, only the FIRST-listed turtle homed — as \"a defensible reading of an ambiguous spec ... escalated to the maintainer rather than resolved here\". That escalation is answered: the maintainer ruled on issue #738 that `clear_screen` wipes the canvas once and sends EVERY addressed turtle home, and spec/turtles-and-sprites.md:111/:113 were amended accordingly (PR #861). The order-dependence the old name recorded (\"current\" == the first member of the addressed set, so `tell [ :b :a ]` homed :b instead) is therefore gone — clear-screen-homing-order-independent pins that directly. Every change of the addressed set — here from `tell`/`each` — also emits the addressing `primitive` event carrying `addressing: { addressed_turtle_ids, current_turtle_id }`, so a consumer can identify the addressed turtle set (issue #766, spec/rendering.md:193); `ask`/`each` emit one again when the previous set is restored, on every exit path.",
